id: design.backpressure
kind: design
title: Backpressure
aliases: backpressure, bounded work, flow control
tags: architecture, reliability, concurrency
questions:
  - what happens when producers are faster than consumers?
  - how do you stop a queue from growing forever?
invariant: When producers outrun consumers, the system must push back, drop, or buffer -- an unbounded buffer is not a solution, it is a delayed failure.
answer_30s: Backpressure is the mechanism that makes producers slow down when consumers cannot keep up: bounded queues, finite permits, and explicit shedding instead of unbounded growth.
answer_2m: |
  Every queue has three possible responses to overflow: block the producer
  (backpressure), drop work (shedding), or grow (which converts a throughput
  problem into a memory problem and moves the failure in time rather than
  removing it). The engineering question is which one is correct at that
  boundary, and the answer differs per stage: a user-facing ingest endpoint may
  reject with 429, an internal worker should block and slow its source, a
  metrics pipeline can drop samples silently. Backpressure is therefore a chain
  property, not a component property: if one stage has an unbounded buffer, the
  pressure never reaches the source, and the failure appears as an OOM far from
  the cause. It is also the reason HPA alone is not an answer -- autoscaling
  reacts in minutes, backpressure in milliseconds.
when_to_use:
  - any async hand-off: HTTP to worker, worker to database, service to service
avoid_when:
  - genuinely lossy data (metrics samples) where load shedding is cheaper than blocking
commands:
  - kubectl top pod => is memory growing in lockstep with ingress rate, the unbounded-queue fingerprint
failure_modes:
  - unbounded channel or in-memory queue hiding the overload
  - backpressure applied at the wrong layer, so the failing stage is not the one that slows down
  - HPA as the only overload response, too slow to prevent the OOM
misuse:
  - add more workers without bounding the queue => moves the bottleneck and multiplies the blast radius
follow_ups:
  - where would you drop work instead of slowing down?
related:
  - design.bounded-queue
  - design.semaphore
  - design.load-shedding
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
  - rust.bounded-concurrency
  - k8s.hpa
sources:
  - https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/
  - https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-insurmountable-queue-backlogs/
